In article <6i6eq1$c85$1@agate.berkeley.edu>,
Raymond Fellers <rsf@lotus.Berkeley.EDU> wrote:
>I have a fast Linux Alpha box and a very slow Digital Alpha box. I'm doing
>alot of number crunching with Fortran so I've been compiling my code on the
>DU box with the libs statically linked then running the code on the Linux
>box. What I don't know is will Linux run DU executables as fast as running
>from DU, i.e. does Linux incur any additional overhead or are execution times
>identical?

>Raymond Fellers


I do this too, except with C++ binaries.  The statically-linked 
binary seems to run just as fast on the Linux/Alpha box as it 
should.

I've actually performed timings, comparing a 
600MHz PWSAU running digital UNIX and the same binary running on a
500MHz 21164 Linux machine.  

Both had the same amount of cache, but the 500MHz machine had 
the older memory interface:  it uses parity 60ns DRAM, not SDRAM.

The binary ran the same task with these times:
249 seconds on the 600MHz Digital UNIX machine
310 seconds on the 500MHz Linux machine

Timing Ratio:  1.24
Clock speed ratio: 1.2
SPECfp95 ratio: 1.24    (mine is a floating-point intensive code)

So yes, it does seem likely to me that you're getting full
performance on your Linux/Alpha machine.  

Comments:  you might want to use compilation flags to
target optimization for your Linux/Alpha CPU:
I used optimization flags like this with DEC CXX:
cxx -arch ev56 -O3 -tune ev5

Second, if yours is a type of program which makes lots of
OS calls (such as file access, memory allocation, swapping, etc.)
then your performance may differ greatly:  my program
spends very, very little time doing file access or memory
allocation compared to CPU/memory use.

Regards,

PeterM




