xgz@gmsds.ms.ornl.gov (Xiaoguang Zhang) writes:

> 2. The compilers. gcc and g77 generates codes that are much much slower
> than DEC compilers. If you do any numerical applications, get DU.

This advice is overly simplistic. The right thing to do is:

1. compare gcc/g77 to egcs; you may find egcs to be significatly faster.

2. Does your code spend a significant amount of time in math library
   routines? If so, Digital Fortran can be a lot faster, but if not,
   egcs is within 10% to 20% and sometimes can even win.

3. Being from ornl, of course you know that there's a free
   assembly dgemm() routine that's faster than Digital's ;-)

The answer is still often "DF is significantly better", but not always.

BTW, I'd be happy to test other compilers (microway, others?), but
none of 'em have issued SPECmarks, nor will they give me an eval
copy to compute SPECmarks, so I have no idea if they are any good.
Don't buy a compiler if the vendor refuses to run SPEC95cpu.

-- g




