Now I realize that I'm running on a AlphaStation, and not a Jensen,
but I haven't had a single problem with 2.1.98.  I've only added one
patch to my (clean) copy of the kernel sources (the previously posted
NTFS patch), and I've managed to get by without upgrading most of the
rest of the operating system (heck, I think I've only upgraded
modutils and net-tools, in fact).

Aside from the usual growing pains (like that swap thrashing problem
about ten revs back), I've found the devel series remarkably well
integrated on the Alpha.  I have yet to apply any form of
Alpha-specific patches to a development kernel.  Compared to 1.3 and
early 2.0 kernels, this integration of Alpha code is almost miraculous
in my eyes.  A year and a half ago the Alpha port was in its infancy
(literally), and I think it's come along quite nicely since, thank you
very much.  Almost every single Alpha system out there can run Linux
(except for the TurboChannel machines and probably some other exotic
box, like that Tadpole laptop).

Work on performance enhancements in the libraries and compiler is
proceeding (or at least the groundwork is being laid).  The C library
itself has been stable (if not very nearly bug free, at least as far
as I can see) for many months now.  For me, X had a few problems in
the S3 device driver, but the S3 X server in XF86 3.3.2 seems to have
fixed that.

Nils, *almost everything* in Linux/Alpha works!

The developers aren't tired.  They aren't worn out.  The Compaq
buy-out didn't scare anyone off.  If recent newsgroup traffic is any
indication, more and more people are buying Alpha systems and running
some form of GNU/Linux on them (well, everybody except for those math
geeks *wink*).

The only problem with the developers is that none of them, including
<gasp> Linus himself, is omniscient!

It just might be possible that none of the developers has a Jensen, so
bugs that affect that machine (and that machine only) don't get caught
until 2.2.  If you find a bug in 2.1 and know how to fix it, make a
patch and send it in!  That's what a _development_ kernel is for,
Nils, to develop.  If you're running a devel kernel and aren't willing
to hack at it a bit to get it working, then back yourself down to a
stable kernel and wait for 2.2.  THEN complain bloody murder that 2.2
doesn't compile correctly because some idiot changed
arch/alpha/kernel/bios32.c without checking to see if it would
throttle one of the older machines.  :)

In other words, *CHILL OUT*.  Go to bed.  Sleep.  You'll feel better.
(You probably have already, I hope.)  Those up-til-5AM kernel hacking
sessions are just about enough to put anyone into a bad mood.  Don't
sweat it.  Baring nuclear holocaust, we'll all be here tomorrow.

