On 27 Apr 1998 21:23:02 GMT, Ben T. Eisemann <ben@eisemann.com> wrote:
>Today I received my UDB in the mail (cute little machine).  But, was 
>disappointed to read that there is a "known" compatibility problem with seagate 
>hard drives and as they put it "should avoid them at all cost!".  Normally I 
>wouldn't be so worried but I recently bid and won a seagate scsi-2 2.1gb hard 
>drive just for the UDB!!!  I really do not want to put in any more money until 
>I at least get to take the UDB for a spin.   Has anybody worked with the UDB 
>and / or the seagate hard drives and can shed some light on the problem?  

This sounds bogus.

I'd be interested in hearing where you heard about this purported
problem; I've had a Seagate connected to my UDB without any particular
problems. 

>Also, the manual reccomends not using an internal hard drive... i
>would be interested to know what users on this group have implemented
>with their UDB's? 

This, on the other hand, is much better rooted in fact, although it may
still be somewhat misleading.  The problem is that the UDB power supply
was designed to be only just powerful enough to handle the hardware that
comes with the machine.  By default, the machine came with a 340MB 2.5"
hard drive.  You may add a bigger drive, but iff (if and only if) you
remove the 2.5" drive. 

The other problem with a bigger internal drive is that a 3.5" drive will
occupy the space where the PCI expansion slot is.  If you want a better
video card (and the S3 968 is the only one that is clearly documented to
work with the UDB), you can't have that *and* a bigger internal drive.

The engineers from Digital that participate in the Alpha/Linux mailing
list have repeatedly commented that if you hook up a second drive that
this is likely to overcome the power supply.

When I hooked up the Seagate drive, I used the external SCSI port, and
it was powered by another system, so that power was a non-issue.  These
days, I just NFS mount disk over from other machines, so it's also not
much of an issue. 

See: <http://www.hex.net/~cbbrowne/alpha.html> for more details... 

