That's strange.  I thought the decision had been made to stick with
the 4-byte record marks on Linux/Alpha under egcs, but I admit to not
keeping track.

I do know this is a bit of a challenging issue.  Why should records
on Linux/Alpha systems be limited to 2^32 elements?  I don't know
that they should offhand.  How interchangable should unformatted
files be across platforms?  That's another question often hashed
out, but never truly resolved, here.  Certainly libf2c (which g77
uses) doesn't try to resolve endianness in that area -- so you can
probably easily convince (say, with a one-line change) the libf2c
built for g77 on Linux/Alpha systems (under egcs) to use 4-byte,
not 8-byte, record marks, but that only works because the underlying
endianness "happens" to be the same.

