Isn't that 2018 thing a bitch?  It screws up so much stuff.  Very,
very annoying, and no way to avoid it during installation unless you
set your ARC clock back to 1942.  The later 2.1 kernels (just noticed
it in 2.1.96) seem to try to detect the correct epoch, but I've found
my clock to be reset to 2018 several times because (for whatever
reason) the machine decides to switch back to ARC time and resets the
RTC accordingly.

Down, not across.

No, SRM isn't any better.  Just more direct, if you have it.  I have
an old firmware CD (version 3.7) because for a while I was playing
around with OpenBSD.  After a while, I decided to re-install Linux,
and rather than re-install Microsoft's ARC (and have to deal with the
whole boot->ARC->MILO->Linux crap), I just kept SRM and booted Linux
directly from there.  Note that you have to partition your disk with
minlabel (don't used fdisk in disklabel mode, and I had problems with
minlabel getting it right, so I used OpenBSD's disklabel program
instead...if it isn't one thing, Paul, it's another).

That way, I don't have to mess with a middle-man (ARC), nor do I have
to be worried about finding a combatible version of MILO for my
system.  Keeps things simple (oh, who am I kidding...I did it for hack
value).  Besides, I haven't booted Windows NT on my Alpha for four
months now.  'set os_type UNIX' and 'date 1998...'  and everything
(including installation under 2.0.x kernels) works exactly the way it
should.

...baring the weird signal 11s during installation and file system
corruption when XF86_S3 (version 3.3.1-14) decides to freeze solid.

