The department I work for at Georgia Tech is about to lose our mail and web
servers.  (2 Ultra 1's, Solaris 2.5.1, sendmail for mail serving and Apache
for web serving)  The separtment wants me to find a new solution that has both
the power for now and tomorrow, and is on the cheap side.  We got a quote for
a Ultra Enterprise 450 at around 30K (US$) but they wont spring for that.  My
IT department has considerable experience with x86 Linux as well as
SparcLinux, but little Alpha Linux experience.  A few weeks ago I started
looking at some Alpha boxes from Carerra, Aspen Systems, and VAR.  The pricing
is attractive.  In general, we could get a Alpha 600 or 533, 128 MB of RAM, 9
GB UWScsi, TBU, etc for somewhere in the range of 6 to 8 thousand bucks.  My
question is, can a system like this handle around 300-400 mail accounts with
the users checking their mail every ten minutes, and web serving (around
200,000 hits per month)?  One of our big problems is the amount of mail
traffic with BIG (30MB) attachments.  It can easily choke the Ultra if there
is much other traffic.  I can't seem to find a single networking benchmark
published for such a system, and the vendors haven't been real helpful.  I am
confident that a DG-UX system with the same hardware could do it, but I am not
yet sure about Alpha Linux.  Any ideas or opinions?  Anything, including
pointers for outside resources, would be most appreciated.  Thanks!

