slewis10@hotmail.com wrote:
> 
> looking at some Alpha boxes from Carerra, Aspen Systems, and VAR.  The pricing
> is attractive.  In general, we could get a Alpha 600 or 533, 128 MB of RAM, 9
> GB UWScsi, TBU, etc for somewhere in the range of 6 to 8 thousand bucks.  My
> question is, can a system like this handle around 300-400 mail accounts with
> the users checking their mail every ten minutes, and web serving (around
> 200,000 hits per month)?  One of our big problems is the amount of mail
> traffic with BIG (30MB) attachments.  

> Scott Lewis
> School of Civil and Environmental Engineering
> Georgia Institute of Technology

We have a Microway Screamer 533 PC164 with 512MB ram and 9GB of
hard drive space doing what you are interested in, but maybe not
quite on the same scale.  We use it as a number cruncher, web server,
news server, and mail server.  It gets lots of hits and has people
routinely adding 5-15MB attachments to email and news posts, with 
little trouble.  It only has around 10 - 20 people routinely using
it, though, so I couldn't testify to whether it would definitely
hold up under pressure.  Every once in a while, the network locks
up and the alpha/linux has to be rebooted to free it (say 1 time
per 2 months or so).  That could be a more serious problem for you.

On the other hand, I put it together and completely maintain it,
on my own, based solely on the experience of using Linux on my office
PC for a year.  In other words, I don't know a whole lot about Linux
and still have little trouble.  This group helped me with the few
startup problems I encountered.  If you have an IT dept. with any
Linux experience at all, you are in good shape.

mark pauli
