Sorry, this one didn't make it into the free source. Here's the patch
for ns/nsprpub/pr/include/md/_linux.h:

Index: _linux.h
===================================================================
RCS file: /m/src/ns/nspr20/pr/include/md/_linux.h,v
retrieving revision 2.0
retrieving revision 2.1
diff -r2.0 -r2.1
69a70,73
>
> #if defined(__GLIBC__) && __GLIBC__ >= 2
> #define _MD_GET_SP(_t) (_t)->md.context[0].__jmpbuf[JB_SP]
> #else
70a75,76
> #endif /* defined(__GLIBC__) && __GLIBC__ >= 2 */
>       


> After the before mentioned problem I gave it try with "make -k" but then I
> saw many many more syntax errors due to misdefined or undefined things. Too
> bad!

NSPR (netscape portable runtime) is like libc. If it doesn't compile,
nothing else works.

Also, there is a kernel bug that makes 
ns/nsprpub/pr/test/<bin dir>/threads

core dump. The behavior I saw was that if a signal (SIGALRM) is
delivered  while the process is doing sigprocmask, you get a core
dump. gdb on Redhat 5.0 can't debug this core dump, because it's
buggy. I got a private snapshot from cygnus, which showed that the
process was inside a system call (namely sigprocmask) when it died.

You can get around this problem by setting NSPR_NOCLOCK=1 in your env.

Are you using lesstif to compile ? The Alpha machine we have here is a
bit slow and I haven't compiled the released sources on it yet.

Also, I'm taking a month off starting tomorrow and shall be checking
my mail (adsharma at home.com) only once in a while. Let me know if
you need more info.

	-Arun



